On Thu, 15 Oct 2026 16:30:39 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> This patch is good, and I've accepted it into my ports tree.

Thank you.

> However in looking at the SigNoz deployment guide, it says it requires a
> signoz otel collector to run - this is separate from the signoz binary.

You are right, and the port's text understates it. The collector is
required twice over. Without it nothing reaches ClickHouse, and the
ClickHouse schema is never created.

- Ingestion. The architecture page says the collector "accepts
  telemetry data from diverse sources and writes it to ClickHouse"
  [a]. signoz only queries.
- Schema. The Linux install guide [b] creates the schema with
  "signoz-otel-collector migrate bootstrap", then "migrate sync up",
  then "migrate async up". In signoz v0.145.0, "signoz metastore
  migrate" only touches the SQLite/Postgres metadata store
  (pkg/sqlmigration). Nothing in pkg/telemetrystore migrates
  ClickHouse.
- Configuration. signoz serves OpAMP on 4320. The collector connects
  to it through --manager-config (ws://127.0.0.1:4320/v1/opamp) and
  receives the log pipelines that users edit in the UI.

The schema the query service reads is the one the collector's
migrations write, so the two versions move together.

> Research the docs and source code, and tell me what you find - ideally
> with a plan to build the signoz collector binary as part of this port.

Upstream facts (github.com/SigNoz/signoz-otel-collector):

- The latest release is v0.144.14, from 2026-10-13. Nothing pins one
  version to signoz v0.145.0: its devenv uses v0.142.0, its CI
  v0.144.6, its tests v0.144.9 (with a TODO for v0.144.10), and
  Foundry uses "latest". I would take v0.144.14.
- It is one Go module with go 1.25.0 and 878 requires; signoz has 376.
  The replace directives all point at versioned SigNoz forks
  (ch-go, clickhouse-go, ksuid, grok) or a pinned opencensus, so the
  module proxy serves them all. There is no cgo in its own code.
- cmd/ has two programs. signozotelcollector is the collector, and its
  "migrate" subcommand runs the migrations. signozschemamigrator is
  the older standalone migrator, still released but superseded. Only
  the first is needed.
- It pins github.com/bytedance/sonic v1.14.1, sonic/loader v0.3.0, and
  base64x v0.1.6, the same as signoz. So it hits the same Go 1.26
  build failure and the same mmap(2) panic.
- Upstream ships Linux and darwin tarballs only. Foundry's systemd
  example runs the migrations as a oneshot (migrate ready, bootstrap,
  sync up, async up) and then the collector with "migrate sync check"
  as ExecStartPre. The collector runs with --config, --manager-config,
  and --copy-path; the last defaults to /etc/otel/signozcol-config.yaml.

I built it on this host (FreeBSD 15.1, Go 1.27.2, CGO_ENABLED=0) from
the v0.144.14 module, vendored, with sonic swapped for v1.15.4. Two
vendored packages failed to compile:

- opentelemetry-collector-contrib receiver/filestatsreceiver v0.144.0:
  "undefined: collectStats". The BSD code is in filestats_darwin.go
  with "//go:build darwin || freebsd || ...", but the _darwin file
  suffix restricts it to darwin whatever the build line says. A copy
  named filestats_freebsd.go fixes it.
- github.com/AthenZ/athenz libs/go/sia/util v1.12.13 (pulled in by
  the pulsar exporter): "undefined: UpdateFile" and others. It has
  os_util_linux.go and os_util_darwin.go but no FreeBSD file. A copy
  of the Linux file named os_util_freebsd.go fixes it; the two differ
  only in one log message.

With those two copies, both binaries build. The first run panicked
with "invalid argument" in sonic/loader.mmap, as signoz did before
the port's fix. With fd -1 in mmap_unix.go, the fix from sonic commit
bf12ab8, "signoz-otel-collector --help" and "migrate --help" run
cleanly. The collector is 298 MB stripped, against 428 MB without -s.
Not checked: running it against ClickHouse, the migrations, OpAMP, a
poudriere build, and aarch64.

The plan for one port:

go.mk handles one GO_MODULE. go-post-fetch downloads that module's
graph into ${DISTDIR}/go/${PKGORIGIN}, go-post-extract vendors it
in GO_WRKSRC, and do-build builds GO_TARGET there. The collector
follows the same three steps by hand, using the same module cache:

1. Fetch. Add the collector's v0.144.14.mod and .zip from the module
   proxy as a :collector group, with SIGNOZ_COLLECTOR_VERSION as its
   own variable. A post-fetch target runs "go mod download all"
   against the collector's go.mod into GO_GOPATH, as go-post-fetch
   does for signoz. Its dependencies need no distinfo entries, just
   like signoz's. post-fetch (700) runs before go-post-fetch (800), but
   the two are independent.
2. Extract. The proxy zip unpacks to
   ${WRKDIR}/github.com/!sig!noz/signoz-otel-collector@v0.144.14 (to be
   confirmed on extract). post-extract runs "go mod tidy -e" and
   "go mod vendor -e" there with GOPROXY=${GO_MODCACHE}, offline.
3. Patch. pre-patch symlinks the collector's vendored sonic to
   ${WRKSRC_sonic}, the same tree signoz uses. So the existing
   post-patch line that applies bf12ab8 fixes both binaries, with no
   second tuple. post-patch adds the two _freebsd.go copies with ${CP},
   each with a comment naming the upstream bug. I would report both
   upstream so the copies can go later.
4. Build and install. post-build runs "go build" with
   ${GO_BUILDFLAGS} for ./cmd/signozotelcollector in the collector
   tree, into ${GO_WRKDIR_BIN}. post-install installs it as
   bin/signoz-otel-collector.
5. Configuration. conf/default.yaml mixes DSNs
   (tcp://localhost:9000 and tcp://clickhouse:9000), so like
   example.yaml it needs a short reviewed sample:
   ${ETCDIR}/otel-collector.yaml.sample and
   ${ETCDIR}/otel-collector-opamp.yaml.sample. The latter is upstream's
   manager.yaml, pointing at 127.0.0.1:4320. --copy-path goes to
   /var/db/signoz/otel-collector.yaml, since /etc/otel is outside the
   tree and the collector rewrites that file from OpAMP.
6. rc.d. A second script, signoz_otel_collector, runs as the signoz
   user, as the signoz script does. A "migrate" extra command runs
   ready, bootstrap, sync up, and async up, and start_precmd runs
   "migrate sync check", following Foundry's systemd units. The DSN
   comes from signoz_otel_collector_env, as for signoz.
7. Text. pkg-descr and pkg-message stop saying the collector is
   packaged separately, and pkg-message gives the order: ClickHouse
   with a cluster named "cluster", then "service signoz_otel_collector
   migrate", then start both.

Cost: about 30 Makefile lines that repeat go.mk's fetch, vendor, and
build steps, and that depend on its internal variables (GO_ENV,
GO_GOPATH, GO_MODCACHE). A change to go.mk could break them. Building
and fetching about double, and the package gains about 300 MB. A
collector-only host, which is common for edge ingestion, installs the
query service and web UI too; an OPTIONS knob could split that later.

The other route is a separate net-mgmt/signoz-otel-collector port,
with plain USES=go:modules and the same sonic and _freebsd.go fixes.
net-mgmt/signoz would then RUN_DEPENDS on it. That uses only
documented go.mk features, but the two versions would have to be
bumped together by hand across two ports.

I recommend the single port, as you suggested. The version coupling
through the schema is real, and the fixes are shared. On your go-ahead
I will post it as a patch against net-mgmt/signoz as you accepted it.

